<title>USA: Tele-Communications may drop services - NYT.</title>
<headline>Tele-Communications may drop services - NYT.</headline>
<dateline>NEW YORK 1996-12-05</dateline>
<text>
<p>The chief executive of Tele-Communications Inc fired his first salvo in what could become a battle with cable programmers, the New York Times reported Thursday.</p>
<p>The executive, John Malone, told a small group of analysts on Monday that his company was likely to drop several cable services, including WGN, the Chicago superstation owned by the Tribune Co, and Comedy Central, a cable service jointly owned by Time Warner and Viacom, the Times' story says.</p>
<p>Malone said the services were not profitable or popular enough, the story said.</p>
